747 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The problem is simple: you have an existing class, maybe a third-party library, a legacy system, or just a class you can't modify, that provides functionality you need. But its interface doesn't match what the rest of your code expects.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
748 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Without an adapter, you're stuck. You can't change the vendor's code, and you don't want to mess up your own clean architecture by making it conform to this weird interface. The solution? Write an Adapter class that sits between your client and the vendor class, translating the requests.)
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
